.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# Left behind by FUSE mounts when a file is replaced while still open elsewhere
.fuse_hidden*
**/.fuse_hidden*
*.swp
*~
.vscode/

# Anything secret. Nothing in this project needs credentials — archive logins live
# in the browser profile (see docs/searching.md), not on disk — but if a .env ever
# appears, it must not be the commit that teaches us why this line matters.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# scratch screenshot-harness artifacts (not part of the project)
__mt_host.html
__shot.html
__mt.html
__*.html

# Scratch belonging to an unattended run, not to the project. `/autopilot` writes the
# whole-tree worklist here so ten passes do not each recompute it, and a pass that reads
# acts writes its intermediates alongside. Both are derived, both are large — .tmp-pass5
# was 26 MB of act JSON — and neither is a record of anything.
#
# Ignored because of what the run is told to do next: "one commit per pass", unattended.
# A `git add -A` in that loop commits whatever is lying around, so leaving these visible
# means the first interrupted run quietly puts tens of megabytes of scratch into a
# repository whose whole packed history is 22 MB.
.autopilot-worklist.json
.tmp-pass*/

# The harvested corpus: re-fetchable open data that grows without limit, so it is not
# committed. research/harvest/manifest.json IS committed — it records the exact
# queries, which is what makes the corpus reproducible from the repository alone.
research/harvest/*
!research/harvest/manifest.json

# data/artifacts/ IS committed, images included, and that is deliberate.
#
# The corpus above is excluded because it is re-fetchable: the manifest reproduces it.
# An artifact is the opposite. It is the evidence a record cites, and the validator
# recomputes its sha256 on every build — so a missing file is a hard error, not a
# degraded clone. Excluding the images would leave a checksum with nothing to check and
# a citation pointing at nothing, which is the one failure the artifact design exists to
# prevent. Archive URLs rot; the held copy is the entire point.
#
# It costs 1.3 MB across four scans today. Revisit when it approaches ~100 MB, and the
# answer then is Git LFS or a content-addressed store keyed by the sha256 already in the
# record — not dropping the evidence.
#
# The real limit is rights, not size: a scan usually belongs to the holding institution.
# Check the `repository` field before adding one, and if it may not be redistributed,
# keep the record and its transcription and leave the image out — the record still
# carries the URL, the digest and what was read.

# uv's virtualenv. pyproject.toml and uv.lock are committed.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The rendered documentation is NOT ignored, and that is deliberate. mkdocs.yml and
# docs/ are the source; dist/docs is generated by `uv run tools/build.py` (which renders
# it and records dist/docs/build-signature, the file check_data.py compares against)
# and committed for the same reason dist/bundle.js is: Pages serves this repository as
# it stands, so an artefact that is not here is a 404 — and index.html now links to it.
# It is 3.3 MB and byte-stable across rebuilds (MkDocs zeroes the gzip mtime), so a
# rebuild with no content change is an empty diff rather than churn.
#
# The output directory is deliberately NOT the default "site/" — that directory holds
# site/labels.json, and MkDocs deletes its output directory before every build.
#
# Revisit when the docs build moves to CI; at that point both generated artefacts leave
# git together. See the scaling notes in CLAUDE.md.
#
# The source maps are the exception: 1.2 MB of the 4.3 MB, they exist to debug MkDocs'
# own minified bundle, nobody reading this tree will ever open one, and they change
# whenever the theme is rebuilt. The site works without them.
dist/docs/**/*.map
